WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 216: Spain: The Reconquista, Ferdinand and Isabella, and the Fall of Granada

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.400
[Rebecca] Today we enter the sun-drenched, dramatic history of Spain.

4
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:21.440
[Rebecca] In a single pivotal year, 1492, the centuries-long reconquista reached its triumphant climax, remaking the Iberian Peninsula and launching a world empire.

5
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:30.240
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of Ferdinand and Isabella, the fall of Granada, and the Catholic monarchs.

6
00:00:44.240 --> 00:01:04.000
[George] In a lightning conquest that astonished the medieval world, the Muslim armies shattered the Visigothic kingdom of King Roderic at the Battle of Guadalite, sweeping across the entire Iberian Peninsula in just seven years, advancing deep into France until halted by Charles Martell at the Battle of Tour in 732 AD.

7
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:20.320
[George] Under the Umayyad Caliphate, Muslim Al-Andalis flourished as the intellectual and cultural beacon of medieval Europe, turning its capital of Cordoba into a metropolis of half a million citizens with paved streets, streetlights, public baths, and seventy libraries.

8
00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:31.760
[George] Yet in the rugged, mist-covered picos de Europa Mountains in northern Asturias, a small band of Visigothic refugees led by a nobleman named Pelio refused to surrender.

9
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:41.280
[George] In 722 AD, at the Battle of Cavadonga, Pelio ambushed a large Muslim force inside a steep, rocky mountain gorge.

10
00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:53.520
[George] Hailed as a miraculous Christian victory, Cavadanga ignited the Reconquista, an unyielding, 770-year Holy Crusade to reclaim the Iberian Peninsula for the Christian cross.

11
00:01:53.520 --> 00:02:07.880
[George] The Battle of Cavadanga was small in military scale but colossal in symbolic power, establishing the Kingdom of Asturias as a sanctuary of Christian Visigothic resistance that nurtured the 7th century crusade of the Reconquista.

12
00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:23.960
[George] The Battle of Cavadonga, fought in the rugged limestone gorges of the Picos de Europa in Asturias, became the foundational origin myth of the Spanish Christian kingdoms, transforming a small band of mountain guerrillas into the holy vanguard of national reconquest.

13
00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:33.400
[George] Over the next seven centuries, the Reconquista was not merely a military campaign, but a defining way of life that shaped Spanish national character.

14
00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:51.160
[George] The frontier between Christian kingdoms in the north, Asturias, Leon, Castile, Navarre and Aragon, and Muslim Taifas in the south was a fluid, violent zone of border raids, fortified watchtowers called Castillos that gave Castile its name, and chivalric romance.

15
00:02:51.160 --> 00:03:03.080
[George] In the 11th century, this frontier gave birth to Spain's supreme national hero, Rodrigo Dias de Vivar, immortalized as El Cid Campidor, the master of the battlefield.

16
00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:17.240
[George] A brilliant knight who fought both for and against Christian and Muslim rulers with equal pragmatic brilliance, El Cid captured the wealthy coastal kingdom of Valencia in 1094, ruling it with justice and tolerance until his death.

17
00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:30.280
[George] In July 1212, at the decisive Battle of Las Navas de Telosa in Andalusia, a coalition of Christian kings under Alfonso Vii of Castile broke the military spine of the Almohad Caliphate.

18
00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:44.440
[George] City by city, Cordoba in 1236, Seville in 1248, fell to Christian armies, until Muslim rule was compressed into a solitary, fortified redoubt in the southern mountains, the Emirate of Granada.

19
00:03:44.640 --> 00:04:02.240
[George] El Cid's famous sword, Tizona, became a revered national relic of Spanish chivalry, celebrated in the medieval epic poem Cantor de Mio Cid, which celebrated his unmatched military valor, devotion to his wife Jaimna, and unwavering loyalty to his king.

20
00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:21.120
[George] The Spanish frontier was a crucible of continuous cultural and military friction, where Castilian knights, Mozarabic Christian scholars, and Jewish merchant translators intermingled, producing a vibrant blend of Romanesque architecture, Mudajar brickwork, and Arabic scientific translations.

21
00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:29.360
[George] By the mid-15th century, Christian Spain remained divided, paralyzed by weak rulers and aristocratic civil strife.

22
00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:41.920
[George] But in October 1469, in a private mansion in Valladolid, an 18-year-old princess and a seventeen-year-old prince executed a clandestine marriage that would alter the destiny of Europe.

23
00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:53.120
[George] Princess Isabella of Castile, possessing an iron will and deep Catholic piety, had rejected the arranged diplomatic marriages proposed by her half-brother, King Henry IV.

24
00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:59.280
[George] Instead, she chose Ferdinand, the handsome, militarily shrewd crown prince of Aragon.

25
00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:10.160
[George] Because they were second cousins, they forged a papal dispensation to marry, with Ferdinand travelling across hostile territory in disguise as a merchant's servant to meet his bride.

26
00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:20.240
[George] When Henry IV died in 1474, Isabella seized the throne of Castile in a daring coup, proclaiming herself queen in the main square of Segovia.

27
00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:24.480
[George] Ferdinand succeeded to the throne of Aragon in 1479.

28
00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:39.320
[George] By uniting Castile, the vast agricultural heartland, with Aragon, the wealthy maritime empire dominating Barcelona, Valencia, Sicily and Naples, the Catholic monarchs created a unified Spanish superpower.

29
00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:54.360
[George] The union of Ferdinand and Isabella laid the institutional foundation of the modern Spanish state, establishing the Santa Hermindad, a royal nationwide police force that cleared highways of banditry and curtailed the lawlessness of feudal barons.

30
00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:58.600
[George] El Cid's military genius lay in his flexible pragmatism.

31
00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:08.360
[George] Exiled by his jealous king, he entered the service of the Muslim Amir of Zaragoza, fighting with equal brilliance under the banner of the crescent as under the cross.

32
00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:12.360
[George] Becoming an immortal embodiment of Spanish martial chivalry.

33
00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:25.800
[George] Determined to forge absolute political and religious unity across their fractured realms, Ferdinand and Isabella instituted one of the most feared and controversial judicial institutions of history, the Spanish Inquisition.

34
00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:32.120
[George] Established by Papal Bull in 1478, the Spanish Inquisition was unique in Europe.

35
00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:39.640
[George] It was not controlled by the Pope in Rome, but was directly subordinate to the Spanish crown as an instrument of royal policy.

36
00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:50.200
[George] In 1483, the monarchs appointed Isabella's personal confessor, a severe Dominican friar named Tomas de Torquimada, as Grand Inquisitor of Spain.

37
00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:55.240
[George] The primary target of the Inquisition was not foreign heretics or practicing Jews.

38
00:06:55.240 --> 00:07:09.080
[George] But the Conversos, hundreds of thousands of Jewish converts to Christianity and their descendants who had converted under duress during pogroms a century earlier, whom the Crown suspected of secretly practicing Judaism in their homes.

39
00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:25.200
[George] Operating in absolute secrecy, accepting anonymous accusations, and utilizing judicial torture like the strapado and the water boarding toca to extract confessions, the Inquisition staged public ceremonies of penance called autos dafe acts of faith.

40
00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:34.880
[George] Those convicted of unrepentant heresy were handed over to the secular authorities to be burned at the stake, casting a dark pall of religious surveillance across Spain.

41
00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:50.800
[George] The Spanish Inquisition established tribunals across Castile and Aragon, utilizing regional palaces and monasteries to conduct inquiries, while Grand Inquisitor Torquimada standardized procedural handbooks that governed investigations across the Spanish world.

42
00:07:50.800 --> 00:08:05.840
[George] The Union of Aragon and Castile brought together the maritime Mediterranean ambitions of Barcelona and the sprawling, militant agrarian expanse of the central Iberian Meseta, forging a powerhouse state capable of dominating continental Europe.

43
00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:17.680
[George] With their domestic authority secured, Ferdinand and Isabella turned their military power toward the final, unfinished business of the Reconquista, the conquest of the Nasrid Emirate of Granada.

44
00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:32.160
[George] Nestled against the snowcapped Sierra Nevada Mountains, protected by rugged gorges and thirty fortified stone citadels, Granada had maintained its independence for two and a half centuries by paying heavy annual gold tribute to Castile.

45
00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:41.280
[George] But in 1481, when the Moorish Emir seized the Christian border town of Zahara, the Catholic monarchs resolved upon total conquest.

46
00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:44.880
[George] What followed was a brutal, ten year war of attrition.

47
00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:58.320
[George] Ferdinand commanded the field operations, deploying the latest military technology, massive siege cannon and bronze bombards transported on widened mountain roads that pounded medieval stone walls into powder.

48
00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:09.400
[George] Isabella managed the logistics with flawless organization, establishing the first modern military field hospitals with medical tents, doctors and beds for wounded soldiers.

49
00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:31.480
[George] When an accidental fire destroyed the Christian military camp in 1491, Isabella demonstrated her unshakable resolve by ordering the construction of an entire permanent stone city with grid streets and defensive walls in just eighty days, naming it Santa Fe, holy faith, demonstrating to the besieged Muslims that the Christian army would never leave.

50
00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:46.680
[George] The siege of Granada was the first European military campaign where artillery bombards determined the outcome of siege warfare, as Christian master gunners pounded ancient stone towers into rubble, forcing the Nasrid defenders into eventual surrender.

51
00:09:46.680 --> 00:10:03.480
[George] The Santa Fe military encampment outside the walls of Granada was constructed of solid stone and brick after an accidental fire destroyed the Queen's royal tent, demonstrating to the besieged Moors that the Catholic monarchs would never abandon the siege until the city surrendered.

52
00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:10.440
[George] Inside the walls of Granada, the population was starving and hopelessly divided by bitter dynastic feuds.

53
00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:18.440
[George] The young Amir Mohammed XIII, known to Spanish history as Boabdil, recognized that further resistance was suicide.

54
00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:25.320
[George] On the freezing morning of January 2, 1492, the ceremonial gates of Granada opened.

55
00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:35.880
[George] Dressed in morning robes, Boabdil rode out to meet Ferdinand and Isabella on the banks of the Jenil River, handing over the massive iron keys to the city and the royal palace.

56
00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:43.560
[George] Boabdil bowed and whispered in broken Arabic, These are the keys of your palace, go and possess it with honor.

57
00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:53.120
[George] The royal procession ascended the hill to the Alhambra, the Red Citadel, the breathtaking pinnacle of Moorish Islamic architecture, with its court of the lions.

58
00:10:53.120 --> 00:11:00.560
[George] Delicate marble columns, intricate stucco honeycomb arches, and fragrant reflecting pools of running mountain water.

59
00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:10.240
[George] At the highest point of the fortress, the Torah de la Vila, the Christian heralds raised the silver crucifix and the royal standard of Castile and Santiago.

60
00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:19.520
[George] Looking upon the cross, Ferdinand and Isabella fell to their knees on the stone terrace, weeping with joy, while the royal choir sang the T Diem.

61
00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:24.240
[George] Seven hundred and seventy years of the Reconquista were over, Spain was whole.

62
00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:40.160
[George] The Alhambra Palace complex in Granada remains the crown jewel of Islamic architecture in Europe, with its delicate stucco inscriptions repeating the Arabic motto, Wa la Galiba il a Allah, there is no conqueror but God, carved upon every wall.

63
00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:53.840
[George] As Boabdil rode southward into exile through the Alpujera Mountains, he stopped at a rocky pass known as the Moor's Last Sigh to look back at the distant towers of the Alhambra and wept, his mother famously rebuking him.

64
00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:57.040
[George] Weep like a woman for what you could not defend as a man.

65
00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:09.200
[George] As Boabdil rode south into exile across the rocky mountain pass overlooking Granada, he stopped his horse and looked back upon the shimmering Alhambra one final time, breaking into bitter tears.

66
00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:16.400
[George] His fierce mother Aiksa rebuked him with immortal words: weep like a woman for what you could not defend like a man.

67
00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:21.920
[George] A mountain ridge known to this day as El Suspiro del Moro, the Moor's last Psy.

68
00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:26.640
[George] The year 1492 was a titanic turning point for Spain and the world.

69
00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:44.920
[George] Flushed with victory, Ferdinand and Isabella issued the tragic Alhambra decree in March 1492, expelling all practicing Jews from Spain within four months, scattering 200,000 people into the Sephardic diaspora across North Africa, the Ottoman Empire, and Europe.

70
00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:54.040
[George] In the royal encampment of Santa Fe, the monarchs held an audience with a persistent, visionary Genoese navigator named Christopher Columbus.

71
00:12:54.040 --> 00:13:02.200
[George] Convinced by Royal Treasurer Louis de Santangel, Isabella agreed to finance Columbus's expedition with three modest caravels.

72
00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:10.200
[George] On August 3, 1492, Columbus sailed from the port of Palos, sighting the Americas on October 12.

73
00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:20.120
[George] In a single miraculous year, Spain had completed the Reconquista, expelled the Moors, and laid the foundation for a global empire on which the sun would never set.

74
00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:37.080
[George] The departure of Christopher Columbus from Palos in August 1492, coinciding with the tragic exodus of thousands of exiled Jewish families from Spanish ports, launched the Maritime Age of Discovery and the transatlantic Spanish Empire that reshaped world history.

75
00:13:37.080 --> 00:13:57.000
[George] The fateful year 1492 simultaneously marked the military completion of Christian Spain, the tragic expulsion of over 100,000 Sephardic Jews who enriched the Ottoman and Mediterranean worlds, and the birth of a colossal global empire across the Atlantic that flooded Europe with silver and gold.

76
00:13:57.320 --> 00:14:04.760
[Rebecca] From the mountain caves of Cavadanga to the surrender of the Alhambra, Spain was forged in the fire of crusade and imperial ambition.

77
00:14:04.760 --> 00:14:09.640
[Rebecca] Next week, we conclude our exploration of Spain with episode 217.

78
00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:10.440
[Rebecca] Philip I.

79
00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:30.080
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

